WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.540
INTRO:
Apple is infamous for being the premium tech

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:11.179
company with insanely thick profit margins.

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:14.860
Many would compare Apple to designer brands
like Gucci and Louis Vuitton that have gross

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.240
margins well over 50%.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:22.599
And when you see products like a $1000 monitor
stand and $700 Apple wheels, it’s not surprising

00:00:22.599 --> 00:00:24.760
that Apple has such an image.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.769
But in reality, Apple has some of the lowest
margins in the tech industry.

00:00:28.769 --> 00:00:33.800
Now, don’t get me wrong here, Apple still
makes boatloads of money with annual earnings

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.740
of $111.85 billion which is the third highest
in the world.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:44.100
But the thing to keep in mind is that Apple
also has revenue to match coming in at $365

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.900
billion per year which is the 4th highest
in the world.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.230
In terms of margin though, Apple’s net margin
has hovered at just above 20% for most of

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:56.040
the past decade, and it wasn’t till the
past 12 months that it upticked to 25% mostly

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.150
due to their services.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:03.650
On the other hand, Google has a net margin
of 30%, Nvidia has a net margin of 32%, Facebook

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:09.049
has a net margin of 36%, and Microsoft has
a net margin of 38.5%.

00:01:09.049 --> 00:01:13.060
And none of these guys hold a candle to Visa
who boasts a net margin of 51%.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:17.479
Yet, the only company that gets hate for overpricing
their products is Apple.

00:01:17.479 --> 00:01:22.249
So, here’s how much Apple actually makes
from their products and why their overpriced

00:01:22.249 --> 00:01:25.610
brand image is really all just marketing.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:31.090
COST OF IPHONE:
The number one reason Apple doesn’t have

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:35.119
net margins as high as the other top tech
companies is because they’re the only one

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:36.750
that’s mostly a hardware business.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.610
They’re the only ones that have to design
a physical product, source materials, work

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:44.299
with manufacturers, package their products,
run physical apple stores, work with retailers,

00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:46.689
ship their products, and so on and so forth.

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:51.180
I don’t think I need to explain why hardware
businesses are far less profitable than software

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.180
businesses.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.009
But then how much exactly does Apple make
from their iPhones and Macbooks?

00:01:56.009 --> 00:02:00.569
Well, luckily for us, Apple is a public company
meaning that they post all of their financials

00:02:00.569 --> 00:02:02.509
on a quarterly basis.

00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:06.069
And if we take a look at their most recent
quarterly report, we’ll see that Apple pulled

00:02:06.069 --> 00:02:08.470
in $65 billion in revenue.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:13.720
Out of this, $38.9 billion was from iPhones,
$9.2 billion was from Macs, $8.3 billion was

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:18.270
from iPads, and $8.8 billion was from accessories
and other devices.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:22.770
As you can see, iPhone sales account for about
60% of Apple’s entire product revenue.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.020
And iPhones are basically the middle of the
road in terms of price.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.730
So, we’ll base our analysis on the iPhone.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:32.780
Apple’s most popular iPhone every year is
likely the standard iPhone.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.940
This year, that happens to be the iPhone 13
which comes in at $800.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.360
With all of that out of the day, let’s look
at Apple’s first expense which is the cost

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:41.660
of sales.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:45.550
Cost of sales is the amount of money Apple
has to spend to produce their products.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:51.800
Last quarter, this came in at $42.79 billion
or 65.7% of their product revenue.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.250
Normalizing this data to the iPhone 13, we
get $526 per phone.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:00.620
So, if you’re able to pick up an iPhone
13 for $200 off from a carrier or retailer,

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.040
you’re getting the phone for pretty close
to cost of production.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.220
Something else to keep in mind is that the
cost of production isn’t the only expense.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.400
The next expense on the list is research and
development.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:16.480
Last quarter, R&amp;D came in at $5.772 billion,
but Apple doesn’t list out how much of this

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.800
is for their products and how much of this
is for their services like Apple music.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.920
With cost of sales, 89% of the cost of sales
went towards the products.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.110
And it’s likely a similar case with R&amp;D.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:30.500
I think we’d agree that the vast majority
of Apple’s R&amp;D is spent on designing their

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.610
products and designing software that is directly
linked to their products like iOS, Face ID,

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.180
Touch ID, and so on.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:40.670
So, I think 89% is a reasonable estimate,
but to estimate conservatively, we’ll call

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.420
it 75%.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:47.900
This means that Apple roughly spends $4.329
billion per quarter on product related R&amp;D.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.210
That works out to 6.6% of product revenue
or $53 per iPhone 13.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:56.370
Moving on, we have selling, general, and administrative
expenses.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:01.030
This includes marketing, the cost to run their
stores, patents, licenses, and basically everything

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:02.980
else involved in running Apple.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:06.570
Last quarter, this came out to $5.616 billion.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:12.190
If we assume a 75/25 split again between products
and services, we get $4.212 billion per quarter.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:17.430
That works out to 6.5% of their product revenue
and $52 per iPhone 13.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.019
And finally, the last expense we have is income
taxes.

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:24.610
Last quarter, Apple paid $2.697 billion in
income taxes.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:28.860
Now, for this category, we have to reverse
our 75/25 assumption.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:32.539
Given that Apple’s services business is
much more profitable than their products business,

00:04:32.539 --> 00:04:35.990
the income tax due to service related revenue
is a lot higher than the income tax due to

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:38.240
product related revenue in terms of percentages.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.150
So, if we assume that only 25% of Apple’s
tax bill is due to product related profits,

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.150
we get $674 million.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:51.560
As a proportion of their product related revenue,
we get 1.07% or about $8 per iPhone 13.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:57.009
All of this works out to a total of $639 per
iPhone 13 leaving Apple with $161 worth of

00:04:57.009 --> 00:04:59.330
profit or almost exactly 20%.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:04.430
If Apple had priced the iPhone 13 at $700,
their net margin wouldn’t even be 10%.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:09.590
So, from a business perspective, I think we’d
agree that $750 is really the lowest they

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:13.419
could’ve gone so $800 is pretty fair in
my opinion.

00:05:13.419 --> 00:05:16.570
Something else to consider is that most of
Apple’s iPhone lineup is actually cheaper

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:17.810
than the iPhone 13.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:24.060
Under the iPhone 13, we have the 13 mini,
the 12, the 12 mini, the 11 and the SE.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.699
And all of these likely only have a net margin
of 10-15%.

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:32.069
On the other end of the spectrum, we have
the 13 pro and the 13 pro max, and those no

00:05:32.069 --> 00:05:35.849
doubt have a profit margin of 30-40% if not
over 40%.

00:05:35.849 --> 00:05:40.080
But, only a very small number of customers
buy these products and they know that they’re

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.529
paying a big premium.

00:05:41.529 --> 00:05:45.629
For the vast majority of iPhone buyers though,
it’s really not that bad.

00:05:45.629 --> 00:05:49.409
Something else to note is that the vast majority
of iPhone buyers aren’t buying at retail

00:05:49.409 --> 00:05:51.719
price through the Apple website.

00:05:51.719 --> 00:05:55.349
Most people either trade in their phones,
get a deal with their carrier, wait for discounts

00:05:55.349 --> 00:05:58.199
at retailers, or they buy older generations.

00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:02.800
If you’re buying smart, it’s the carrier
or retailer that’s footing the $100 to $200

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.440
that goes to Apple’s bottom line.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:08.610
So, the truth is, the average consumer is
not overpaying for iPhones.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:13.009
If that’s the case though, how are there
so many alternative options that are way cheaper

00:06:13.009 --> 00:06:14.099
than Apple's offerings?

00:06:14.099 --> 00:06:19.469
Well, the answer to this is two fold with
the first being alternative modes of monetization.

00:06:19.469 --> 00:06:24.270
ALTERNATIVE MODES OF MONETIZATION
One of the most famous value smartphones out

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.250
there is Google’s pixel lineup.

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:31.870
The Google Pixel 6 is comparable to the iPhone
13, but it comes at $200 cheaper at $600.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:36.020
According to our math, this would leave Google
with a negative profit margin of $39.

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.460
So, how could that be possible?

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:40.169
Well, there’s no secret to it.

00:06:40.169 --> 00:06:41.169
It’s really as simple as that.

00:06:41.169 --> 00:06:44.270
Google is selling their phones for no profit
if not a loss.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:48.879
And while that might sound taboo, it’s actually
extremely popular within consumer tech.

00:06:48.879 --> 00:06:50.919
One of the best examples is gaming consoles.

00:06:50.919 --> 00:06:56.039
In May of 2021, Microsoft straight up admitted
that they’re selling Xboxes at a loss.

00:06:56.039 --> 00:06:59.951
With the Xbox and the pixel series, the money
is made after the sale, not during the sale.

00:06:59.951 --> 00:07:04.050
The vast majority of Google’s profit still
comes from Google.com and their number one

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:07.110
goal with their smartphones is to get you
to use Google.com.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:11.950
Now, Apple does have methods to monetize users
after the initial purchase as well, but the

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:14.379
key difference is that all of them are voluntary.

00:07:14.379 --> 00:07:18.569
You’re not forced to buy Apple Music or
iCloud and you never have to purchase an app

00:07:18.569 --> 00:07:19.809
from the app store.

00:07:19.809 --> 00:07:23.150
With the Pixel, however, unless you actively
avoid Google’s services, they’re gonna

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:26.580
serve you ads, they’re gonna collect your
data, and they’re gonna make money.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.870
Now, I’m not trying to get into the morality
of this or claim that one mode of monetization

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:31.870
is better than the other.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:35.999
All I’m trying to illustrate is that with
Apple, users have to actively choose to spend

00:07:35.999 --> 00:07:38.669
more money for Apple to make more money.

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:43.229
But with Google, users have to actively avoid
Google’s services for Google to not make

00:07:43.229 --> 00:07:44.229
money.

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:47.330
Considering this, Apple has to make their
money upfront while many competitors don’t.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.150
And that’s one of the key reasons for Apple’s
inflated prices.

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:53.300
Also, for the record, Google has a higher
net margin than Apple, so technically you’re

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:55.439
overpaying more for a Pixel than an iPhone.

00:07:55.439 --> 00:07:58.189
It’s just not out of pocket.

00:07:58.189 --> 00:08:02.330
HIGHER COST OF BUSINESS
The other key reason that Apple products are

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:05.900
oftentimes more expensive than the competition
is because they spend more money on all aspects

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.289
of their business starting with their employees.

00:08:08.289 --> 00:08:12.119
Now, Apple does manufacture their iPhones
in China using extremely cheap labor just

00:08:12.119 --> 00:08:13.119
like the rest of the competition.

00:08:13.119 --> 00:08:16.710
So, it’s not like Apple is a saint, but
in terms of white collar workers, Apple pays

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:17.999
them extremely well.

00:08:17.999 --> 00:08:23.189
The median compensation at Apple is $159,000,
but that’s actually weighed down by retail

00:08:23.189 --> 00:08:25.080
employees and customer support.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.220
If we look at departments like business development,
communications, product development, sales,

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:32.390
and even HR, the average salaries are above
$200,000.

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:37.380
Some of the biggest companies that destroy
Apple in terms of value are Xiaomi and Huawei,

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.940
but these companies are based in China.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:44.410
Now, these companies may be excellent employers
and they may be paying way above average for

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:45.459
Chinese standards.

00:08:45.459 --> 00:08:50.279
I’m not familiar with Chinese salaries,
so I can’t tell, but compared to American

00:08:50.279 --> 00:08:53.070
standards, the compensation is extremely low.

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:56.290
At Xiaomi for instance, a software engineer
earns 216,000 yuan.

00:08:56.290 --> 00:09:00.540
While that might sound like a lot, it’s
actually only $33,800.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.860
Now, you wanna guess what Apple pays their
software engineers?

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:09.720
The minimum Apple pays their software engineers
is $162,000, and that number actually goes

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:12.940
up to $768,000 probably for chief engineers.

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:16.480
I think that’s all that needs to be said
about employee compensation.

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.060
Aside from higher compensation, Apple has
expenses that many competitors completely

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:21.300
avoid.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:25.190
Apple has 515 stores around the world, Google
has 1.

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:28.910
Also, Apple is the one that often develops
new technologies like fingerprint readers,

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:31.170
Face ID, and wireless headphones.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:35.070
Of course, they didn’t create these technologies,
but they’re the ones that created the form

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:37.190
that we use today.

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:41.390
Competitors often just copy it from Apple
which significantly reduces their own R&amp;D

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:42.540
costs.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:46.800
OVERPRICED PRODUCTS
If this is all true, then why in the world

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:50.540
does Apple come out with braindead products
like $700 Apple wheels.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.060
Well, the answer is simple, it’s just marketing.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:57.300
How many people do you think actually buy
these ludicrous products?

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:00.250
The answer is no one, and Apple is well aware
of that.

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:04.160
They don’t stock these products at their
stores, and if you order it online it takes

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:05.160
forever to ship.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.270
The Pro Stand, for example, takes 15 to 17
days to ship to my house.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:13.430
The iPhone 13, however, which is likely in
way more demand can ship to my house tomorrow

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:14.589
for free.

00:10:14.589 --> 00:10:18.649
You would think that if I’m buying a $1000
monitor stand with 90% profit margins, I should

00:10:18.649 --> 00:10:20.470
get like 1 hour delivery.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:25.120
But, instead it's over 2 weeks because Apple
doesn’t give a crap about these products.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:28.410
These products are specifically designed to
create headlines and to get people to meme

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:33.149
about them and spread the word that Apple
is a luxury status brand even though they’re

00:10:33.149 --> 00:10:34.149
not.

00:10:34.149 --> 00:10:36.710
Now, don’t get me wrong here, Apple makes
outstanding products.

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:39.080
But, they’re more like Seiko than Rolex.

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.630
They’re marketing, however, would convince
you otherwise.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:44.779
So, don’t fall for the headlines.

00:10:44.779 --> 00:10:48.019
Apple does not overprice the vast majority
of their products.

00:10:48.019 --> 00:10:50.750
The reason their products are more expensive
is because they have to make their profit

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.850
upfront and their business costs more to run.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:57.079
At the end of the day though, they only take
10 to 20% to the bottom line when it comes

00:10:57.079 --> 00:10:58.279
to their consumer products.

00:10:58.279 --> 00:11:01.140
Do you guys still think that Apple products
are overpriced?

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:02.350
Comment that down below.

